  the target revision's `bts entry-intent --config ~/.bts-orchestrator.toml`: it must print `enter` and exit 0. Then `bash scripts/cron-setup-hetzner.sh show` and `install`.</li><li>The snapshot is from October: stop the scheduler, run `bts season-contract revoke --season 2027 --reason "restored from the offseason snapshot"` (it sets aside any manual observation and last season's saver flag), then `bts season-contract status`.</li><li>Each day until the post-enable `bts fetch-contest-streak` succeeds and `bts season-contract status` reports `automatic path alone: accepted` for the active ET season: `bts set-contest-streak --date <today> --streak N --best-streak B --saver S` with N, B and S read from MLB. While the automatic path is refused, a day without a usable dated observation has no pick. The refusal names this command. **Enter it in the morning, before the scheduler's first lineup check.** The scheduler reads the state when it starts the day (10:00 ET, or an hour before an early first game) and DMs `Morning check for <date>: no usable contest state was found at startup …` with the time of the first lineup check (change set 6b-1; no morning DM when that check is under ten minutes away, as on a 06:10 opener). An observation entered or corrected AFTER the day's pick was chosen does not re-pick by itself: the earlier pick is refused at delivery (change set 5a) and a new one is chosen only if a later lineup check or the fallback refresh still runs and its prediction succeeds. If none remains, that day has no pick. **Do not restart the scheduler to force a re-pick**: a restart can run two prediction passes and miss the cutoff, sleeps to the regular check on a thin slate, and mid-send leaves the day locked as unconfirmed (`build/cs5b-check/contract-check.md`).</li><li>Let `bts fetch-contest-streak` run (it records readings in the ledger only). When `bts season-contract status` shows readings that match MLB for some days: stop the scheduler, wait for any fetch, `bts season-contract enable --season 2027`, run `bts fetch-contest-streak`, then check that `bts season-contract status` reports `automatic path alone: accepted` for the active ET season. If the fetch fails or automatic status is refused, follow the diagnostics, keep filing daily dated observations and restart live scheduling with today's accepted dated observation while cutover is pending. If interrupted, verify fetch completion and current status before stopping daily observations. After the first successful, accepted automatic fetch, read `bts saver-state` for the active ET year. Initialize only if it reports `uninitialized`, using the actual MLB saver state with `bts saver-state --init <MLB state> --season <ET year>`. Recheck the ET day and year before acting on earlier output. If a concurrent fetch initializes the flag first, reread it. Restart the scheduler.</li><li>**Expected false alarms in the first days (ruling R39; do not chase them):** `park_drag_freshness` pages `CRITICAL` at the end of Opening Night and until the first 07:45 refresh that sees a game (the table's last game is last September); and if the season is switched to automatic within its first two days, the contest-state health check can page `CRITICAL` for a missing `source_date` until MLB's ledger shows the first settled pick. Anything else, or either of these lasting past the third day, is real. A third expected alarm, only if Opening Night's pick locks while one team's lineup is still unposted (a thin early lock, ruling R18): the live-forward research capture for that date fails its row count (nine rows where ten are expected), `analytics_artifacts_missing` pages `CRITICAL`, and the resolution alert for that date warns from day 4 and is `CRITICAL` from day 7 without ageing out (ruling R41). It costs that one date's research capture and no pick; to quiet it, ask Claude to archive that date's capture directory by hand.</li><li>After the switch: a pick delivered on an automatic reading whose last accepted fetch is more than 14 hours old still goes out, with one `STALE CONTEST READING` warning DM that day (at the morning check if it is already stale then, otherwise after the pick is delivered; change set 4c) and a record on the pick file (`automatic_reading`). On that DM: check MLB; if the streak or saver differs, enter the day's dated observation (before the first check if it is the morning DM); then find out why the fetch stopped (`bts fetch-contest-streak`, its own failure DMs, cookies).</li></ul> Do not run `bts schedule --dry-run` on the box as a probe: it writes the heartbeat and can idle. | Eric runs the box commands; Claude prepares them | `bts entry-intent` exit 0; `status` output before `enable`; post-enable fetch exit 0, `automatic path alone: accepted` for the active ET season, and the active-year saver read; the first day's morning DM (or the journal line `Morning state check: dated contest state accepted`) and the pick after the dated observation |
